%I A401602 #17 Nov 08 2026 20:40:32
%S A401602 0,1410,4200,115986,1575228,90496134,1252055946
%N A401602 Number of Eisenstein primes reachable from 0 by steps of length at most sqrt(A003136(n)), stepping only on Eisenstein primes.
%C A401602 Let w = (-1+sqrt(-3))/2. The norm of a + b*w is a^2 - a*b + b^2.
%C A401602 The vertices are 0 and all Eisenstein primes.
%C A401602 Two vertices are joined if they are at most sqrt(k) apart, where k = A003136(n).
%C A401602 a(n) is the number of primes in the connected component of 0.
%C A401602 Only k in A003136 matter, since the squared distance between two Eisenstein integers is always in A003136.
%C A401602 0 is not counted.
%C A401602 A prime and all its copies under units and conjugation are counted, so a(n) is a multiple of 6.
%C A401602 This is the Eisenstein version of the Gaussian moat problem (Gordon, 1962).
%C A401602 If some term were infinite, one could walk to infinity on Eisenstein primes with steps of bounded size.
%C A401602 West (2014) counts only the primes of the component in the sector 0 <= arg(z) <= Pi/6.
%C A401602 For k = 3, 4, 7, 9 his counts agree with the sector counts of the components here.
%C A401602 For k = 12 and 13 he gets 7541649 and 104339028, but the components here have 7541648 and 104339027 primes in that sector.
%C A401602 Two separate programs gave these values.
%C A401602 For k = 3, 4, 7, 9, 12, 13 the farthest primes in that sector are 52 + 7*w, 92 + 9*w, 535 + 49*w, 2593 + 1120*w, 20973 + 3518*w, 80976 + 2827*w.
%D A401602 E. Gethner, S. Wagon and B. Wick, A stroll through the Gaussian primes, Amer. Math. Monthly 105 (1998), 327-337.
%D A401602 R. K. Guy, Unsolved Problems in Number Theory, 3rd ed., Springer, 2004, Section A16.
%D A401602 P. P. West and B. D. Sittinger, A further stroll into the Eisenstein primes, Amer. Math. Monthly 124 (2017), 609-620.
%H A401602 Ellen Gethner, Stan Wagon and Brian Wick, <a href="https://doi.org/10.2307/2589708">A stroll through the Gaussian primes</a>, Amer. Math. Monthly 105 (1998), 327-337.
%H A401602 Siddharth Prasad, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.2310">Walks on Primes in Imaginary Quadratic Fields</a>, arXiv:1412.2310 [math.NT], 2014.
%H A401602 Karan Sharma, <a href="/A401602/a401602.png">Components of 0 for k = 3, 4, 7 and 9 (colored by number of steps from 0)</a>
%H A401602 Karan Sharma, <a href="/A401602/a401602_1.txt">Table of n, k, a(n), sector count, farthest prime a + b*w, its norm and search depth for n = 2..8</a>
%H A401602 Nobuyuki Tsuchimura, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/ietfec/e88-a.5.1267">Computational results for Gaussian moat problem</a>, IEICE Trans. Fundamentals E88-A (2005), 1267-1273.
%H A401602 Philip P. West, <a href="https://hdl.handle.net/10211.3/141937">A Computational Exploration of Gaussian and Eisenstein Moats</a>, Master's thesis, California State University Channel Islands, 2014.
%H A401602 Philip P. West and Brian D. Sittinger, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4169/amer.math.monthly.124.7.609">A further stroll into the Eisenstein primes</a>, Amer. Math. Monthly 124 (2017), 609-620.
%H A401602 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaussian_moat">Gaussian moat</a>.
%e A401602 k = 1: the six points at distance 1 from 0 are units, not primes, so a(2) = 0.
%e A401602 k = 3: step 1 reaches the 6 primes of norm 3. Step 2 reaches 18 more (6 of norm 4, 12 of norm 7). Step 3 reaches 24 more (norms 13 and 19). The walk ends after 39 steps with 1410 primes.
%o A401602 (Python)
%o A401602 from sympy import isprime
%o A401602 def is_eisenstein_prime(x, y):  # x + y*w, w = (-1+sqrt(-3))/2
%o A401602     if isprime(x*x - x*y + y*y):
%o A401602         return True
%o A401602     q = abs(x) if y == 0 else abs(y) if x == 0 else abs(x) if x == y else 0
%o A401602     return q % 3 == 2 and isprime(q)
%o A401602 def a(k):  # Eisenstein primes reachable from 0 with steps of length <= sqrt(k)
%o A401602     steps = [(dx, dy) for dx in range(-k, k+1) for dy in range(-k, k+1)
%o A401602              if 0 < dx*dx - dx*dy + dy*dy <= k]
%o A401602     seen, todo = {(0, 0)}, [(0, 0)]
%o A401602     while todo:
%o A401602         x, y = todo.pop()
%o A401602         for dx, dy in steps:
%o A401602             v = (x+dx, y+dy)
%o A401602             if v not in seen and is_eisenstein_prime(*v):
%o A401602                 seen.add(v)
%o A401602                 todo.append(v)
%o A401602     return len(seen) - 1
%o A401602 print([a(k) for k in (1, 3, 4, 7, 9)])
%Y A401602 Cf. A003136, A055664, A055667, A345435.
%Y A401602 Cf. A055025, A185271 (Gaussian primes).
